{
  "id": "COMPOUND:24902 - PROTEIN:6324",
  "verbalization": "Phyllofolactones F appears to be a target of multiple compounds that ultimately lead to the activation of M-phase phosphoprotein 8. The evidence suggests that Phyllofolactones F directly interacts with T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3, which in turn is targeted by 2-(1a,4-dimethyl-3,3a,4,5,6,7,7a,7b-octahydro-2H-naphtho[1,2-b]oxiren-7-yl)prop-2-enoic acid and Traxillagenin. Both of these compounds are implicated in the regulation of the M-phase of the cell cycle, as they target M-phase phosphoprotein 8. The presence of multiple pathways converging on M-phase phosphoprotein 8 suggests a potential mechanism involving the modulation of cell cycle progression through the interaction of Phyllofolactones F with CD3 and its downstream targets. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of CD3 and its associated proteins in the regulation of M-phase phosphoprotein 8, potentially through the activation of signaling cascades that control cell cycle progression.",
